   "69aea708971abe996d21b4aa_00471": {
    "donor": "6953ea3e1479a63c1108641e_00241",
    "donor_arm": "base",
    "text": "Economy,\n\nBy using the best ingredients, you will easily prepare it: the whole should be divided, as if into large olives, and one of these should be taken around the second hour, the other around the tenth hour, and no one will suffer from hunger. Another composition includes Attic sesame in the semisextary measure, and half a part of honey, a cotyle of oil, and sweet almonds of the chœnic type. The sesame and almonds should be dried, ground, and rubbed. Then the scillas should be gathered around, the outer layers removed, the roots and leaves cut off, and the parts divided into small pieces, placed in a mortar, and crushed until they are very fine. Then, the crushed scillas should be mixed with an equal part of honey, along with the oil, and poured into a pot, and placed on top of charcoal. The small pieces of wood should be stirred from side to side until everything is mixed. When the mass becomes solid, it should be divided into small balls, and one taken in the morning, the other in the evening, and this will provide sufficient nourishment. This remedy is useful for the army: for it is sweet, and it fills the stomach and removes thirst. We have taken this from a note in a manuscript in the library of the Vatican, in the book of Heron. We also saw the same composition in Philoponem, in the fifth book on military matters, where he describes similar things.\n\n---\n\n->*From which fruits can wine be made?*<-\n\n### CHAPTER XXI.\n\nNow we will consider the fruits from which wine can be made, but first those made by our ancestors, with a double difference: for one kind is used for remedies, of which the books of the physicians speak, and the other is used for various and nearly infinite uses, according to the differences of places and peoples: for what is given to one is denied to another. First\n\n->*Wine from palms*<-\n\nPlinius said that wine can be made from palms in the East, and he listed fifty kinds of palms, and the different kinds of wine made from them. The cariotæ are most celebrated among them, with the richest juice, from which the chief wines of the East, called iniqua capiti, are made, from which the name \"pomo\" comes. The most praised in Judea is Hiericunte, although it is also found in Archalde and Phaselide, and"
   }
